A||N||5||SeanyD||4 cylinder motors||||1||
Z||000000||SeanyD||07-02-2000||04:50 PM||||I've been told that you can pull almost any 4 cylinder, 2.5L motor out of most any car and it should theoretically fit right in. is this true or is it only for motors out of pontiacs, and if you can yoink one out of a newer pontiac, do you need the computer too even if the engine is carberated??||||reg||1
Z||000001||ChadMan||07-03-2000||08:53 AM||||The following is a combination of experience, expert testimony, and hearsay, probably with a spot or two of BS thrown in, so take it with a grain of salt:<P>All 84-86 Fiero Iron Dukes (2.5 L 4-bangers, 92 hp) are interchangeable.  84-86 Dukes from other GM FWD cars will bolt right into a Fiero, but require the Fiero's intake/exhaust manifolds and accessory brackets.  Keep your stock computer when performing one of these swaps.<P>All 87-88 Fiero Iron Dukes are interchangeable.  87-88 Dukes from other GM FWD cars will bolt in, but you face the same minor difficulties as listed above.  Keep your stock computer.<P>RWD Dukes of any year (from S-10's, early F-bodies, etc) aren't worth the trouble it takes to make them work in a Fiero because of different routing of coolant tubes, accessories, etc.  <P>I think all pre-89 Iron Duke heads will work on our blocks.  There is a Forum member who has a head from an early Iron Duke (Citation, maybe?) on his 84 Fiero block though.  It does not have the swirl-port combustion chamber but he said all the bolt patterns were identical.  He also said the early heads were less prone to cracking.<P>It is a major pain in the a$$ to put an 87-88 Duke in an 84-86 Fiero and vice-versa, because of the differences between distributor and distributorless ignition.<P>The last generation Iron Duke (89 or 90 til 95) made 110 hp @ 5200 rpm.  I have been able to find no information yet on how this engine differs from the 87-88 DIS Duke found in the Fiero, so I'm not sure how easily this would swap in.  I think the exhaust ports are different on the last-gen Duke (D-shaped?) so you'd probably need the exhaust manifold from the donor car as well as the computer - I'm sure the fuel mapping and ignition are different - that extra 12 hp has to come from somewhere.  I'd definitely forget this option if you have an 84-86 Fiero.<P>Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-03-2000||10:08 AM||||The 77 to 81 2.5L used in sunbirds, monza's and skylarks had better webbing in the block. It was real easy to make it 4-bolt mains. The older blocks were stronger and heavier. Very simular to the SD4 blocks in design. The engines use to put out 105hp with a 2bbl carb. You can use the stronger blocks and get the hp to around 150 reliably but there is some machine work required to use the 84 thru 89 heads. We had a guy in the club who used the older block with SD4 parts (head, rods,pistons,and crank) and had a dyno'd 180hp. Cost to make the motor was close to $8000. He said the expensive part was all the machine work required.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||07-03-2000||12:17 PM||||I have also heard that the S-10 truck block is stronger than the passenger car block. Don't know how it compares to the early car blocks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SeanyD||07-03-2000||01:50 PM||||thanks guys, I'll have to look further into it and maybe have a chat or two with the local auto wrecker and see what he has to say!!<BR>Thanks again!||||reg||1
Z||000005||86SE||07-03-2000||03:06 PM||||I got an '81 Pontiac Phoenix with the 2.5L 2 barrel carb, I never knew it had a whole 105 HP!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
